{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789827363,"nonce":"G8yO2+pS73gLeb2V0SCvBnopBeU=","title":"A trace lists compiled and interpreted frames out of order when they interleave","message":"trace_sites listed the compiled frames from the native walk, then the stub-bridge callers, then every interpreter frame. That order is the stack's only while nothing interleaves; once compiled code reaches an interpreted callee through the bridge (common since 1253621 made the resolver answer null in hybrid mode), the compiled frames landed above the interpreted callee. haxe.CallStack trims a fixed number of its own frames from the top, so the capturing line disappeared and CallStack.hx / Exception.hx internals showed instead: unit.hl run whole in hybrid mode failed testExceptionStack on 11 assertions (per-case isolation passed, because the promotion timeline differs).\n\nFix: every interpreter frame records an address inside its native activation (native_sp), the unwinder reports each compiled frame's CFA (and the x86-64 Linux frame chain its rbp), and the two lists merge by address, inner first. Falls back to the old order where a walk has no addresses (Windows, the backtrace() path). TestTraceBridge is the parity case.","files":null}]}